INSERT INTO `SERVMASK_PREFIX_options` VALUES (330,'rr_home_faq','a:5:{i:0;a:2:{s:1:\"q\";s:75:\"What additional costs are generally involved when buying off-plan in Dubai?\";s:1:\"a\";s:796:\"Costs can vary by project and by developer. As a general guide, buyers often budget for:<br><br><strong>• DLD registration fee:</strong> Generally 4% of the purchase price, usually paid via Oqood registration. This is generally the main government registration fee, not an additional fee on top of Oqood.<br><br><strong>• Oqood and admin fees:</strong> Often around AED 4,020 for DLD admin, plus small knowledge and innovation fees. These amounts may change.<br><br><strong>• Developer admin fee:</strong> Varies by developer and project. Some developers may waive it as part of a launch offer. Ask for it in writing before booking.<br><br>Your instalments after booking are generally paid according to the payment plan in your Sales and Purchase Agreement (SPA). Always check your own SPA.\";}i:1;a:2:{s:1:\"q\";s:96:\"What additional costs are generally involved when buying a ready or secondary property in Dubai?\";s:1:\"a\";s:1132:\"For a completed resale property, buyers often budget for:<br><br><strong>• DLD transfer fee:</strong> Generally 4% of the purchase price.<br><br><strong>• Trustee office fee:</strong> Often AED 2,000 plus VAT for properties below AED 500,000, and AED 4,000 plus VAT for properties at AED 500,000 and above. These amounts may change.<br><br><strong>• Title deed issuance:</strong> Often around AED 580.<br><br><strong>• Agency commission:</strong> Often around 2% of the price plus 5% VAT on a secondary purchase. The exact commission should be agreed in writing before transfer.<br><br><strong>• Developer NOC fee:</strong> Varies by developer and community. Your agent can help you confirm the current amount before transfer.<br><br><strong>• Mortgage costs, if financed:</strong> Mortgage registration is generally 0.25% of the loan amount plus AED 290 admin, plus bank valuation and processing fees, subject to the bank.<br><br>As a general guide only, many cash buyers budget around 7% to 8% on top of the price for a resale purchase. Your actual total will depend on your property, your financing and your contract.\";}i:2;a:2:{s:1:\"q\";s:64:\"How are off-plan payments generally handled? Are they protected?\";s:1:\"a\";s:646:\"Under Dubai Law No. 8 of 2007, registered off-plan projects are generally required to have a dedicated escrow account with an approved UAE bank.<br><br>Your payments are generally required to go into that project escrow account, not into an agent personal account or a general developer account. The account is generally named in your SPA. Before you pay, verify the escrow account details in your SPA and on the DLD project record, and always keep your payment receipts.<br><br>Escrow is designed to reduce risk. It does not remove all investment risk. Always check the developer track record and project status, and consider independent advice.\";}i:3;a:2:{s:1:\"q\";s:76:\"Is it possible to get a UAE visa or Golden Visa by buying property in Dubai?\";s:1:\"a\";s:963:\"It may be possible, subject to approval and current authority requirements. A purchase does not automatically give you a visa.<br><br><strong>10-Year Golden Visa:</strong> You may be eligible if you own property worth AED 2 million or more. This may be one property or a portfolio in your name. Mortgaged property may qualify with a bank letter or NOC confirming at least AED 2 million has been paid, subject to current requirements.<br><br><strong>2-Year Property Investor Visa:</strong> If you are the sole owner of a completed property, there may currently be no minimum property value requirement. If the property is jointly owned, each investor may need to hold a share worth at least AED 400,000 to qualify, subject to current requirements.<br><br>Visas are not guaranteed with purchase. You must still meet medical, insurance, documentation and authority approval requirements. Confirm your eligibility before booking if a visa is a main reason for buying.\";}i:4;a:2:{s:1:\"q\";s:64:\"How can I generally verify a developer and project before I pay?\";s:1:\"a\";s:560:\"Before booking, consider checking:<br><br>• Whether the project is registered with DLD and has an active escrow account.<br>• Whether the developer name, project name and escrow account in your SPA match the DLD record.<br>• The developer track record for delivery, quality and handover.<br>• The payment plan, handover date, grace period and resale or NOC terms in the SPA.<br><br>A genuine agent should be willing to share the SPA, project registration details and escrow information before asking for payment. Do not rely only on marketing material.\";}}','off');
