INSERT INTO `SERVMASK_PREFIX_posts` VALUES (30,0,'2026-02-24 09:00:00','2026-02-24 09:00:00','<p class=\"po-lede\">A 60/40 and an 80/20 plan are not the same deal, even when the headline price is. Read the payment plan as a cash flow, not a discount.</p>\n<h2>What the percentages mean</h2>\n<p>The first number is what you pay before handover, the second what you pay after. A 60/40 plan asks for 60% during construction; an 80/20 asks for 80%. The higher the pre-handover share, the more of your money is at risk for longer, and the less you have left to furnish or rent the place.</p>\n<h2>Clauses worth negotiating</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Handover delay compensation.</strong> Ask what happens if the developer is late, and get it in writing.</li>\n<li><strong>Post-handover instalments.</strong> Usually quarterly, usually with a fixed schedule. Check whether they carry a fee.</li>\n<li><strong>Early payment discount.</strong> Sometimes a real saving, sometimes a trap that empties your reserve.</li>\n<li><strong>Resale during construction.</strong> Check whether the developer allows it and at what fee.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>Model the worst case: handover six months late and a tenant arriving three months after that.</blockquote>\n<h2>My rule of thumb</h2>\n<p>Keep at least 10% of the purchase price aside for DLD fees, agency, furnishing and the gap before rent starts. If the payment plan leaves no room for that, the plan is too aggressive for you.</p>','Off-plan payment plans, read line by line','A 60/40 and an 80/20 plan are not the same deal. What the post-handover percentage really costs you, and the clauses worth negotiating.','publish','open','open','','off-plan-payment-plans','','','2026-02-24 09:00:00','2026-02-24 09:00:00','',0,'https://bagheeraventures.com/realtorraihan/off-plan-payment-plans/',0,'post','',0);
INSERT INTO `SERVMASK_PREFIX_posts` VALUES (31,0,'2026-11-19 07:14:04','2026-11-19 07:14:04','','penthouse-terrace-dusk.webp','','inherit','open','closed','','penthouse-terrace-dusk-webp','','','2026-11-19 07:14:04','2026-11-19 07:14:04','',30,'https://bagheeraventures.com/realtorraihan/off-plan-payment-plans/penthouse-terrace-dusk-webp/',0,'attachment','image/webp',0);
INSERT INTO `SERVMASK_PREFIX_posts` VALUES (32,0,'2026-03-04 09:00:00','2026-03-04 09:00:00','<p class=\"po-lede\">Gross yield is the number on the brochure. Net yield is the number in your bank account. The gap is service charge, management, vacancy and chiller.</p>\n<h2>The arithmetic</h2>\n<p>Gross yield is annual rent divided by purchase price. Net yield subtracts the costs of owning: service charge, property management if you use it, chiller, insurance, and an allowance for the weeks the unit sits empty.</p>\n<h2>A Business Bay example</h2>\n<p>A studio bought at AED 1.1 million renting at AED 75,000 a year shows a 6.8% gross yield. Take out a AED 14,000 service charge, 5% management, an 8% vacancy allowance and chiller, and the net figure lands nearer 4.6%. Still respectable, but it is a different number from the one that sold you the deal.</p>\n<blockquote>Ask for the net figure in writing before you reserve.</blockquote>\n<h2>Where the spread is widest</h2>\n<p>Buildings with heavy amenities, district cooling and short-let neighbours carry the biggest gap between gross and net. Older, simpler buildings often look duller on paper and pay better in practice.</p>','Gross yield is a sales number. Net yield is the real one','The gap between the two is service charge, management, vacancy and chiller. On a Business Bay studio that spread is wider than most buyers expect.','publish','open','open','','gross-vs-net-yield','','','2026-03-04 09:00:00','2026-03-04 09:00:00','',0,'https://bagheeraventures.com/realtorraihan/gross-vs-net-yield/',0,'post','',0);
INSERT INTO `SERVMASK_PREFIX_posts` VALUES (33,0,'2026-11-19 07:14:04','2026-11-19 07:14:04','','gen-wcm-villa.webp','','inherit','open','closed','','gen-wcm-villa-webp','','','2026-11-19 07:14:04','2026-11-19 07:14:04','',32,'https://bagheeraventures.com/realtorraihan/gross-vs-net-yield/gen-wcm-villa-webp/',0,'attachment','image/webp',0);
INSERT INTO `SERVMASK_PREFIX_posts` VALUES (34,0,'2026-02-16 09:00:00','2026-02-16 09:00:00','<p class=\"po-lede\">Overpricing costs more than a lower asking price. A listing that sits for three months tells buyers something, and it is not flattering.</p>\n<h2>Start from transactions, not asking prices</h2>\n<p>Asking prices are opinions. Registered transactions are facts. I pull comparable sales from the last six months in your building and the two nearest, adjust for floor, view, condition and furnishing, and land on a range.</p>\n<h2>The cost of waiting</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Days on market.</strong> After about six weeks, enquiries drop sharply.</li>\n<li><strong>Price cuts.</strong> Buyers assume a second cut is coming and wait.</li>\n<li><strong>Carrying costs.</strong> Service charge, mortgage and lost rent keep running.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>Price to the market, present it properly, and let competition do the work.</blockquote>\n<h2>What actually moves a sale</h2>\n<p>Correct pricing, good photographs, a clear floor plan, service charge transparency and a seller who can move quickly on paperwork. That is most of it.</p>','Pricing a listing so it sells, not so it sits','Overpricing costs more than a lower asking price. How I set a number from comparable transactions rather than from hope.','publish','open','open','','pricing-a-listing','','','2026-02-16 09:00:00','2026-02-16 09:00:00','',0,'https://bagheeraventures.com/realtorraihan/pricing-a-listing/',0,'post','',0);
INSERT INTO `SERVMASK_PREFIX_posts` VALUES (35,0,'2026-11-19 07:14:05','2026-11-19 07:14:05','','gen-office-dusk.webp','','inherit','open','closed','','gen-office-dusk-webp','','','2026-11-19 07:14:05','2026-11-19 07:14:05','',34,'https://bagheeraventures.com/realtorraihan/pricing-a-listing/gen-office-dusk-webp/',0,'attachment','image/webp',0);
INSERT INTO `SERVMASK_PREFIX_posts` VALUES (36,0,'2026-02-02 09:00:00','2026-02-02 09:00:00','<p class=\"po-lede\">Choosing a community matters more than choosing a building. You can renovate an apartment; you cannot move the school run.</p>\n<h2>Start with the daily routine</h2>\n<p>Where do you work, where do the children go to school, and how often do you leave the city? A 25 minute commute and a 55 minute one are different lives, and the difference usually shows up in the price.</p>\n<h2>The five questions I ask every buyer</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Budget, including fees.</strong> Not just the price.</li>\n<li><strong>Timeline.</strong> Ready now, or is 2028 acceptable?</li>\n<li><strong>Use.</strong> Live in it, rent it long term, or short let it?</li>\n<li><strong>Exit.</strong> Who will buy this from you in five years?</li>\n<li><strong>Non-negotiables.</strong> View, floor, parking, school, pet policy.</li>\n</ul>\n<blockquote>If a community fails two of the five, it is not your community, however good the photos look.</blockquote>\n<h2>Where I would look first</h2>\n<p>For a first home in Dubai I usually start with Business Bay, JVC or Dubai Hills depending on budget and school needs. For yield I look harder at Arjan, Dubai South and JVC. For capital preservation, established waterfront and Downtown still hold.</p>','Choosing a community before you choose a building','You can renovate an apartment, you cannot move the school run. The five questions I ask before we look at a single listing.','publish','open','open','','choosing-a-community','','','2026-02-02 09:00:00','2026-02-02 09:00:00','',0,'https://bagheeraventures.com/realtorraihan/choosing-a-community/',0,'post','',0);
